course: adult-life
lesson: 40
title: Being a good grown-up
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Being a grown-up is not only paying bills and going to work; it is also about what kind of person you are when things are hard.
explain: Good grown-ups keep their promises, say no kindly when they have too much, ask for help, own up to mistakes, stay kind when stressed, spot tricks and look after the people who need them.
explain: Vivid example: in Christchurch, Dad has had a horrible day and it's pouring, but he sighs, "I promised Uncle Pete I'd help him move his sofa, so I'll go." He comes back soaked and grumbling, and Uncle Pete rings to say, "Your dad's a legend."
explain: Grown-ups also live through changes they cannot stop: getting older, parents slowing down, friends drifting away and children leaving home. Good grown-ups face these with honesty and love.
fact: Being reliable means doing what you said you would, even when you no longer feel like it; people trust those who keep their word.
fact: The secret of reliable people is to only promise what they can really do.
fact: Grown-ups must sometimes say no, or they do too much and get exhausted; a kind, clear no is better than a yes that never gets done.
fact: Friendships change as people move, start jobs and have families; some fade, new ones grow, and old friends can sometimes pick up again as if no time had passed.
fact: Asking for help is a grown-up skill; people think better of someone who asks than of someone who hides a problem until it explodes.
fact: Being kind when you are stressed and in a hurry, like letting a car in during a traffic jam, is harder than being kind when things are easy.
fact: A scam is a fake message trying to trick people into giving away passwords or money; real banks never ask for your password by email or text.
fact: Many grown-ups look after their own parents and their children at the same time; it is loving but tiring, and small help takes some of the load off.
fact: Everyone gets older: grey hair, glasses and stiffer knees come, but so do experience and wisdom.
fact: When a grandparent is "slowing down", they may need more help, more doctor visits and more company from their family.
fact: Leaving home for work or study is a normal step in growing up; parents feel proud and sad at once, and home is still home.
fact: Everyone makes mistakes; owning up quickly makes them easier to fix, while hiding them tends to make them bigger.
exercise: Dad sighs, "I promised, so I'll go." What is he showing? | He is reliable and keeps his word | He loves helping more than anything | He has nothing better to do tonight | He is scared of breaking the house rules
exercise: What is the secret of being reliable? | Only promise what you can really do | Promise everything people ask for | Never make any promises at all | Keep promises only when it's sunny
exercise: Mum says on the phone, "I'm sorry, I can't take that on right now." What is she doing? | Saying no politely because she has too much | Saying yes but very quietly so nobody hears | Being rude to the person calling | Asking them to ring back tomorrow
exercise: Why is a kind, clear no often better than a yes? | A yes that never gets done lets people down more | Saying no always makes people like you more than saying yes | A no means you never have to work again | People only remember the word no
exercise: Dad says, "I haven't seen my old school friends in years." Which is true? | Friendships change as people grow up and move | Dad must have had a fight with all of them | Grown-ups are not allowed to have friends | His school friends have all forgotten him
exercise: After weeks of struggling, your big brother says, "I asked my boss for help." What do people usually think of that? | They think better of someone who asks for help | They think he's bad at his job and lazy, and will soon be sacked | They think he should have quit instead | They think help is only for children
exercise: Stuck in a traffic jam, Mum still lets another car in. Why is this special? | Being kind when stressed is harder than when it's easy | Letting cars in is the law in jams | She wants the other driver to pay her back for the favour later | She didn't notice the car was there
exercise: Dad says, "That email's a scam, it's not really from the bank." What is a scam? | A fake message trying to trick you out of money or passwords | A real message from the bank about your money that you must answer today | A message that was sent to the wrong person | A computer virus that breaks your screen
exercise: What do real banks never do? | Ask for your password by email or text | Send letters about your account to your home | Let you pay with a card | Have a phone number you can ring
exercise: Mum looks after Gran's shopping and pills as well as ours. What is this like for her? | Loving but tiring | Easy and relaxing all day | Boring and pointless | Fun and very well paid
exercise: How can you help a parent who looks after everyone? | Take a small job off her hands | Ask for more things to cheer her up | Stay out of her way for the whole week | Tell her to stop helping Gran so much
exercise: Dad finds a grey hair: "I'm getting old!" Which is true? | Everyone gets older, and it brings wisdom too | Only unlucky people ever get grey hair, and nobody knows why | Grey hair means someone is very ill | Getting older stops at about forty
exercise: Mum says after a visit, "Grandad's slowing down a bit." What might he need more of? | Help, doctor visits and company | Fast cars and loud music | Fewer visits so he can rest all day long | A new job to keep him busy
exercise: Your big cousin leaves for a job in another city and Auntie waves, crying. How does Auntie feel? | Proud and sad at the same time | Angry that he's leaving her | Glad to have him out of the house | Worried he'll never speak again
exercise: Uncle says, "I messed up at work, so I told my manager straight away." Why was that wise? | Owning up quickly makes mistakes easier to fix | His manager would never have noticed anyway | Telling makes the mistake disappear entirely | Managers give prizes for making mistakes
test: Dad, soaking wet: "Bloody rain. Still, told Pete I'd help, didn't I." Why did he go? | He keeps his promises even when he doesn't feel like it | He loves carrying sofas in the rain | Uncle Pete was paying him a lot of money for the whole afternoon | He wanted to get out of the house
test: Your mate promises to come to five parties on the same day. What's the problem? | He's promising what he can't really do | Nothing, more promises means more friends | Parties are never on the same day | He should promise ten to be safe
test: Mum, to the school: "Sorry, I can't run the cake stall this year, I'm stretched too thin." Is Mum being lazy? | No, she is saying a kind, clear no so she isn't exhausted | Yes, grown-ups must always say yes to the school whenever they are asked | Yes, she just doesn't like cakes | No, she is too busy eating the cakes
test: Dad, laughing on the phone: "Gaz! Twenty years, mate, it's like we never stopped talking!" What is happening? | An old friend picking up again after years apart | A stranger on the phone pretending to know Dad | Dad being told off by his boss | A scam call trying to steal money from Dad's bank account
test: Big brother hid a problem at work for months until it exploded. What would have been better? | Asking for help early | Hiding it for even longer | Quitting before anybody found out | Blaming a colleague for it
test: Text: "URGENT: your bank account is locked. Click here and type your password." What should you do? | Not click it, and tell a grown-up; it's a scam | Click it fast before the account closes for good tonight | Reply with your password to be safe | Send it on to all your friends
test: Your little sister says, "Mum's always tired cos she's lazy." Put her right. | She's tired from looking after Gran and us too | Yes, she just likes sleeping | No, she's tired because she's ill | Yes, grown-ups get lazy as they get older, everybody knows that
test: Big sister groans, "Dad's got grey hair, he's practically dead." Put her right. | Grey hair is a normal part of getting older | Yes, grey hair means he's very ill | No, he must have dyed it grey | Yes, he's too old to do anything now
test: Mum, after the hospital: "Grandad's not as quick on his feet these days, we'll pop round more." Why will they visit more? | He needs more help and company as he gets older | He has asked them to help him move house to the seaside | He has won some money to share | He wants them to do his work for him
test: Auntie at the station: "So proud of him, but the house will be so empty." How does she feel? | Proud and sad at once | Only happy that he's gone | Only angry at the station | Only worried about the train
test: Your mate broke the class plant pot and hid it behind the bin. What would a good grown-up advise? | Own up quickly so it's easier to fix | Hide it better so nobody finds it | Blame the class hamster for it | Wait until the end of term to say
exam: Identify the principal reason for which reliable persons restrict their undertakings to those they are able to fulfil. | So that every promise made may in fact be kept | So that they are relieved of all further obligations | So that others are prevented from asking favours | So that promises need never again be made in writing
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning electronic messages purporting to originate from a bank and requesting the recipient's password. | Such messages are fraudulent, as genuine banks make no such request | Such messages must be answered within the period stated | Such messages are routine security procedures of every bank | Such messages may be safely answered by children only
exam: Whereas an employee commits an error in the course of his duties, determine the course of action that this lesson commends. | Prompt disclosure of the error to his manager | Concealment of the error until it is discovered | Attribution of the error to a colleague | Resignation prior to discovery of the error
exam: Ascertain the emotions most commonly experienced by a parent upon the departure of an adult child for employment in a distant city. | Pride and sadness simultaneously | Relief and indifference simultaneously | Anger and resentment exclusively | Fear and guilt exclusively
